{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Mirror inventory finding F12 (`reports/stability/mirror-inventory.md`), HIGH, **ALREADY DRIFTED**.\n\n## Problem\n\nTwo masters for one fact — which subcommands the CLI has and what they exit:\n\n- `src/rebar/_cli/_registry.py` `ROUTES` — 78 live routes, **zero aliases**\n- `docs/exit-codes.md:117` Subcommand table — 81 hand-maintained rows\n\n`docs/exit-codes.md:5-7` claims the file is \"the single source of truth ... pinned by\n`tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_exit_codes.py`, which fails if the codes drift.\" The entire\nassertion touching the file (`:236-243`) is\n`assert \"\\`11\\`\" in text and \"block-but-retryable\" in text.lower()`. Nothing checks the table.\n\n## Measured drift — larger than the inventory recorded\n\nThe inventory recorded one addition and one stale row. Verified against the live CLI, it is\n**four stale rows and one missing route**. Every spaced spelling in the table is NOT invocable\n(`ROUTES` has no aliases; the CLI takes one hyphenated token):\n\n| doc row | `rebar <row> --help` | reality |\n|---|---|---|\n| `bridge fsck` (`:120`) | `Error: unknown subcommand` | real route is `bridge-fsck` |\n| `bridge check-access` (`:165`) | `Error: unknown subcommand` | real route is `bridge-probe` |\n| `bridge setup` (`:177`) | `Error: unknown subcommand` | no such route at all |\n| `review` (`:184`) | `Error: unknown subcommand` | removed shim, absent from `ROUTES` |\n| — | — | `bridge-status` is a live route with **no doc row** |\n\nSo a document that calls itself load-bearing for agents instructs them to run four commands that\nerror out, and omits one that works. A count comparison finds none of this: 81 rows vs 78 routes\nwould look like a simple off-by-three, and the inventory's own equal-count reading was wrong.\n\n## Approach — add-census-assertion-to-existing-test\n\nSet-difference the table rows against live `ROUTES` names inside `test_exit_codes.py`, and correct\nthe five discrepancies.\n\nPlain set equality is the correct invariant here, and that is a FINDING rather than an assumption:\n`ROUTES` exposes no aliases, and every spaced spelling was confirmed non-invocable, so there is no\nlegitimate alternate-spelling space to allow for. No allowlist is needed or wanted — one would\nre-admit exactly the fictional rows this fixes.\n\nThis does not collapse a distinct concept (epic AC2): the table's Subcommand column and `ROUTES`\nalready denote the same set — the invocable subcommands — and the table's extra columns (exit\ncodes per command) stay hand-authored and unconstrained.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: `test_exit_codes.py` asserts the Subcommand-table row set equals the live non-retired\n      `ROUTES` name set, comparing real parsed values rather than re-listed literals. Verified by\n      the test passing after the doc is corrected.\n- [ ] AC2: the assertion fails in BOTH directions and names the offenders. Verified by two\n      mutation checks — an extra doc row, and a route removed from the compared set — each RED\n      with the offending name in the message.\n- [ ] AC3: the four non-invocable rows are corrected: `bridge fsck` -> `bridge-fsck`,\n      `bridge check-access` -> `bridge-probe`, `review` removed, and `bridge setup` removed or\n      replaced by its real spelling if one exists. Verified by `rebar <name> --help` succeeding\n      for every remaining row's name.\n- [ ] AC4: `bridge-status` gains a row documenting its exit behavior. Verified by AC1's equality.\n- [ ] AC5: the false pinning claim at `:5-7` is corrected to describe what the test now actually\n      enforces. Verified by reading it against the assertions.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `python -m pytest tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_exit_codes.py -q` — the census assertion and\n  its drift cases (AC1, AC2), green only once the doc is corrected (AC3, AC4).\n- `for c in <every row>; do rebar $c --help; done` — proves no documented row is non-invocable\n  (AC3); the census test encodes this so it does not rely on a manual sweep.\n- `make lint && make typecheck`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788376317995622001,"uuid":"722fdbad-83d0-4401-a9f5-78792f23c465"}